  "configuration_id": "config_009",
  "reason": "Evidence: All four completed experiments are singleprofile with no aggregation. RNA is the dominant signal (rna_only 0.5203 vs image_only 0.2266), and the multimodal gain over RNA alone is modest (~1.7%). Weighted fusion slightly outperforms concat (0.5375 vs 0.5321). The multiprofile + aggregation dimension is completely untested and represents the largest source of remaining uncertainty. Question: Does moving from singleprofile to multiprofile with avgpool aggregation improve performance when keeping the same encoders (scvi_n200, imagenet_vit_l) and fusion (weighted) as our best result (config_004)? Why this is the best next experiment: config_009 is a minimal, clean extension of config_004—only the profile setting and (necessarily coupled) aggregation change. It directly tests whether temporal/multi-profile information adds value on top of the already-strong RNA signal, without confounding the result with a new encoder or fusion strategy. If it improves, multiprofile+aggregation becomes a promising direction to exploit; if it does not, we can rule out that dimension and focus budget on encoder choice or other factors."
}

